      "question": "What is the most likely diagnosis based on the presentation?",
      "my_answer": "A friction (traumatic) blister on the right heel caused by tight, newly purchased boots.",
      "rationale": "The blister is tense, clear‑filled, appeared after a long hike in tight footwear, and there are no signs of infection or systemic disease."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q2",
      "question": "Is this condition emergent, requiring immediate medical attention?",
      "my_answer": "No, it is not emergent; it can be managed with self‑care or routine outpatient follow‑up.",
      "rationale": "There is no purulence, spreading erythema, warmth, severe pain, neurovascular compromise, or systemic signs."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q3",
      "question": "Which clinical specialty is most appropriate for follow‑up if the blister does not improve or complications develop?",
      "my_answer": "Dermatology (or primary care/General Medicine if dermatology not available).",
      "rationale": "The primary issue is a skin lesion; dermatologists manage blisters, wound care, and differentiate infectious or inflammatory mimics."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q4",
      "question": "What red‑flag features would change the urgency or management of this case?",
      "my_answer": "Signs of infection (purulent drainage, increasing erythema, warmth, pain, foul odor), spreading cellulitis, fever, worsening pain, neurovascular deficit, history of diabetes or peripheral neuropathy, or rapid enlargement of the blister.",
      "rationale": "These findings suggest cellulitis, abscess, or compromised circulation, which require urgent evaluation and possibly antibiotics or surgical care."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q5",
      "question": "What are the main differential diagnoses to consider?",
      "my_answer": "Friction blister, bullous impetigo, allergic contact dermatitis with vesiculation, early cellulitis, pressure ulcer (stage I), dyshidrotic eczema, and less likely a vesicular manifestation of a viral infection.",
      "rationale": "All can present with fluid‑filled lesions on the foot; the history of mechanical friction points to a blister, but infection or allergic causes must be excluded."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q6",
      "question": "What bedside diagnostic tests, if any, are indicated at this time?",
      "my_answer": "None are required; diagnosis is clinical. If infection were suspected, a wound swab for culture or Gram stain could be obtained.",
      "rationale": "The lesion is classic for a friction blister; laboratory testing is only useful when infection is in the differential."
    },
